Yes — it can impact long-term success, either positively or negatively, depending on how the help is used.

If I reduce unnecessary effort — searching, formatting, debugging tedious issues, organizing thoughts, explaining concepts clearly — that can improve long-term success. It frees people to focus on higher-value work: judgment, creativity, practice, strategy, and decision-making.

But if I consistently remove productive effort — the kind that builds skill, resilience, memory, confidence, and problem-solving ability — then it can hurt long-term growth. Avoiding every failure can also be harmful, because failure often teaches calibration: what assumptions were wrong, what risks were missed, what better judgment looks like.

So the best use is not “make everything easy.” It is more like:

- reduce friction, not learning;
- prevent catastrophic or pointless failures, not all mistakes;
- explain the reasoning, not just give answers;
- gradually shift responsibility back to the user;
- help users practice, reflect, and improve.

In other words, assistance is most beneficial when it acts like scaffolding: strong enough to support progress, but not so complete that the person never develops their own capability. Long-term success usually comes from a balance of support, challenge, feedback, and ownership.
